    "text": "<p>Complexes in which central atom has unpaired electrons is said to be paramagnetic in nature and where unpaired electrons are not present are diamagnetic in nature.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>CN</mi></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo></math>,&nbsp; &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8594;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msup></math>,&nbsp;paramagnetic&nbsp;(one&nbsp;unpaired electron)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>CN</mi></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></msub><mn>6</mn></msup><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></msub><mn>0</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;,diamagnetic&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Ti</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>CN</mi></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfe